\label{ch:type-erasure}

The representation of typed values at runtime is one of the most consequential decisions in compiler design. It determines memory layout, calling conventions, garbage collection strategy, and the efficiency of type operations. This chapter examines the spectrum of representation strategies---from full erasure to full reification---and the trade-offs each entails.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 52.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Erasure for Parametric Polymorphism}\label{sec:erasure-poly}

Parametric polymorphism guarantees that a function behaves uniformly at all types. This uniformity enables \emph{type erasure}: the compiled code does not depend on the type parameter and can therefore be shared across all instantiations.

\subsection{The Erasure Function}\label{sec:erasure-function}

\begin{definition}[Erasure Function]\label{def:erasure}
The erasure function $\lfloor \cdot \rfloor$ maps typed terms to untyped terms:
\begin{align*}
  \lfloor x \rfloor &= x \\
  \lfloor \lambda x : \tau.\, e \rfloor &= \lambda x.\, \lfloor e \rfloor \\
  \lfloor e_1\; e_2 \rfloor &= \lfloor e_1 \rfloor\; \lfloor e_2 \rfloor \\
  \lfloor \Lambda \alpha.\, e \rfloor &= \lfloor e \rfloor \\
  \lfloor e\; [\tau] \rfloor &= \lfloor e \rfloor
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

Type abstractions and type applications erase to nothing. The resulting untyped term is a standard lambda term, executable on any target that supports the untyped lambda calculus.

\subsection{Correctness of Erasure}\label{sec:erasure-correctness}

\begin{theorem}[Erasure Commutes with Reduction]\label{thm:erasure-commute}
If $e \longrightarrow e'$ in the typed calculus, then $\lfloor e \rfloor \longrightarrow^* \lfloor e' \rfloor$ in the untyped calculus.
\end{theorem}

The proof proceeds by case analysis on the reduction rules. Type-beta reductions ($(\Lambda \alpha.\, e)\; [\tau] \longrightarrow e$) erase to zero-step reductions ($\lfloor e \rfloor = \lfloor e \rfloor$), while term-beta reductions commute directly.

\subsection{Limitations of Erasure}\label{sec:erasure-limitations}

Pure erasure cannot support:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type-case.} Branching on the type of a value requires runtime type information.
  \item \textbf{Type-specific optimization.} Using unboxed representations for primitive types requires knowing the type.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic loading.} Loading code that uses types not known at compile time requires type reification.
\end{itemize}

