\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Boxed representation: a variable on the stack holds a pointer to a heap-allocated object containing the value.}\label{fig:boxed-rep}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Advantages:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Uniform representation simplifies the compiler and runtime.
  \item Polymorphic code works without specialization.
  \item Garbage collection can traverse all values uniformly.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Disadvantages:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Every value requires heap allocation (or at least indirection).
  \item Primitive operations require unboxing (loading from the heap).
  \item Cache performance suffers due to pointer chasing.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Unboxed Representation}\label{sec:unboxed-rep}

In an \emph{unboxed} representation, values are stored directly, without indirection. Integers are stored as machine words, floats as IEEE 754 values, and so on.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=0.8cm,
  box/.style={rectangle, draw, minimum width=1cm, minimum height=0.6cm, fill=TomeBg, font=\small},
]
  \node[box] (reg) {Register: 42};
  \node[below=0.3cm of reg, font=\small] {Unboxed integer 42};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Unboxed representation: the value is stored directly in a register or on the stack, with no indirection.}\label{fig:unboxed-rep}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Advantages:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item No allocation overhead for primitive values.
  \item Primitive operations work directly on machine values.
  \item Better cache performance (no pointer chasing).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Disadvantages:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Different types have different sizes, complicating data structures.
  \item Polymorphic code cannot use unboxed representations (the size is unknown).
  \item Requires type-specialized code generation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Hybrid Approaches}\label{sec:hybrid-rep}

Modern compilers use hybrid approaches:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GHC.} Uses boxed representations by default but unboxes primitive types in specialized code. The \texttt{UNPACK} pragma allows unboxing of fields in data constructors.
  \item \textbf{OCaml.} Uses unboxed representations for integers (with a tag bit) and floats (on 64-bit platforms), and boxed representations for other values.
  \item \textbf{Java.} Uses unboxed representations for primitives (\texttt{int}, \texttt{double}) and boxed representations for objects (\texttt{Integer}, \texttt{Double}).
\end{itemize}

