\section{Coercions for Subtyping}\label{sec:coercions-subtyping}

When subtyping involves a change in representation, the compiler must insert \emph{coercions}: explicit conversion functions that transform values from one representation to another.

\subsection{Numeric Coercions}\label{sec:numeric-coercions}

\begin{example}[Integer to float coercion]\label{ex:int-float-coercion}
If $\mathrm{int} <: \mathrm{float}$, the expression $3.14 + 42$ compiles to:
\begin{verbatim}
  3.14 +f64 cvt_i32_to_f64(42)
\end{verbatim}
The coercion $\mathrm{cvt\_i32\_to\_f64}$ converts the 32-bit integer to a 64-bit float.
\end{example}

\subsection{Structural Coercions}\label{sec:structural-coercions}

For width subtyping on records, the coercion extracts the required fields:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source: { name: String, age: Int, email: String } <: { name: String, age: Int }
  -- Coercion:
  fn coerce_person_to_contact(p: Person) -> Contact {
    Contact { name: p.name, age: p.age }
  }
\end{verbatim}

For depth subtyping, the coercion is applied recursively to each field.

\subsection{Coercion Chains}\label{sec:coercion-chains}

When subtyping involves multiple steps (transitivity), the compiler composes the coercions:

\[
  \mathrm{Int} <: \mathrm{Num} <: \mathrm{Eq} \implies \mathrm{coerce}_{\mathrm{Int} \to \mathrm{Eq}} = \mathrm{coerce}_{\mathrm{Num} \to \mathrm{Eq}} \circ \mathrm{coerce}_{\mathrm{Int} \to \mathrm{Num}}
\]

