\section{Runtime Type Information}\label{sec:rtti-detail}

Runtime type information (RTTI) enables programs to inspect and manipulate types at runtime.

\subsection{Type Descriptors}\label{sec:type-descriptors}

A \emph{type descriptor} is a data structure that describes a type at runtime:

\begin{verbatim}
  struct TypeDescriptor {
    const char* name;
    size_t size;
    size_t alignment;
    TypeDescriptor* parent;       // for subtyping
    void (*destructor)(void*);    // for cleanup
    void (*copy)(void*, void*);   // for cloning
  };
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Uses of RTTI}\label{sec:rtti-uses}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dynamic dispatch.} Virtual method calls use type information to find the correct method implementation.
  \item \textbf{Serialization.} Serializing a value requires knowing its type to determine how to encode it.
  \item \textbf{Debugging.} Debuggers display type information for variables.
  \item \textbf{Reflection.} Programs can inspect and create objects of types determined at runtime.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cost of RTTI}\label{sec:rtti-cost}

RTTI has several costs:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory.} Each object carries a pointer to its type descriptor (typically one word of overhead).
  \item \textbf{Code size.} Type descriptors and type test code increase the binary size.
  \item \textbf{Performance.} Type tests and dynamic dispatch add indirection.
\end{itemize}

Languages that do not need RTTI (e.g., Rust, which uses static dispatch by default) can omit it for better performance.

