\section{Memory Layout Decisions}\label{sec:memory-layout}

The memory layout of data structures is determined by the representation strategy and affects performance significantly.

\subsection{Object Layout}\label{sec:object-layout}

For class-based languages, an object's memory layout typically includes:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Header.} Type tag, GC metadata (mark bit, size), hash code.
  \item \textbf{Fields.} Instance variables in declaration order (or optimized order).
  \item \textbf{Padding.} Alignment padding to ensure proper alignment of fields.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Field Reordering}\label{sec:field-reordering}

Compilers may reorder fields to minimize padding:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source:
  struct S { bool a; int64 b; bool c; int32 d; }
  -- Size: 24 bytes (with padding)

  -- Reordered:
  struct S { int64 b; int32 d; bool a; bool c; }
  -- Size: 16 bytes (minimal padding)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Tagged Pointers}\label{sec:tagged-pointers}

Some systems use \emph{tagged pointers} to encode type information in the pointer itself:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{OCaml.} Integers are tagged with a 1-bit tag (lowest bit = 1), distinguishing them from pointers (lowest bit = 0).
  \item \textbf{JavaScript (V8).} Small integers (Smi) are stored directly in pointer-sized values with a tag bit. Objects are pointers with the tag bit clear.
  \item \textbf{Java (compressed oops).} Object references are 32-bit indices into a heap table, allowing 64-bit JVMs to use less memory for references.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The design of runtime representations has evolved through several generations. Early Lisp systems used boxed representations with type tags. The development of ML and Haskell demonstrated that type erasure could provide efficient compilation of polymorphic programs. The rise of object-oriented languages (C++, Java, C\#) introduced class-based layouts with virtual method tables and RTTI. Modern systems languages (Rust, Go) have revisited the question of representation, using monomorphization and escape analysis to eliminate boxing while retaining high-level abstractions. The tension between uniform representation (for simplicity and polymorphism) and specialized representation (for performance) remains a central challenge in compiler design.
\end{historical}

