\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-52}

The representation of typed values at runtime is a fundamental compiler design decision. Type erasure provides simplicity and uniform polymorphism but sacrifices performance for primitive types. Boxed representations offer uniformity at the cost of allocation and indirection. Unboxed representations provide maximum performance but require type specialization. The practical resolution is a hybrid approach: boxed by default, unboxed where the type is known.

For further reading, we recommend Appel's study of runtime representations~\cite{appel1992}, the GHC documentation on unboxed types~\cite{ghc2024}, and the study of tagged pointers in V8~\cite{v8blog2023}.
